<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chidambaram</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chidambaram is a temple town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> coast. It is famous for the Thillai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a> Temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as the cosmic dancer Nataraja, one of the most sacred sites of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> and later kings endowed and enlarged the temple, whose gilded hall and great gateway towers date from many periods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chidambaram.jpg" alt="Chidambaram" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ryan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chidambaram_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chidambaram, also known as Thillai, is a major town and municipality in Cuddalore district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. It lies on the Vellar River where it meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, and is the headquarters of the Chidambaram taluk. The name is linked to the groves of thillai trees, a mangrove species, that once surrounded the town and temple. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> chit ambara is said to mean the ether of consciousness or the atmosphere of wisdom, in association with Shiva Nataraja, the cosmic dancer.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legend, sages in the Thillai forest believed that rituals and mantras could control the gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> came as a mendicant, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> as Mohini, and the sages created serpents, a tiger and an elephant to attack him. He wore the serpents and the tiger's skin, then invoked the demon Muyalakan, stepped on his back and performed the dance of eternal bliss, showing his true form, and the sages surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town is believed to be of great antiquity. It was ruled at different times by the Pallavas until the ninth century, the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, later Cholas, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, the Thanjavur Nayakas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the British. Sangam literature of the first to fifth centuries refers to the temple or the town. The Thevaram hymns, by the seventh-century poet-saints Thirugnana Sambanthar, Thirunavukkarasar and Sundaramoorthy Nayanar, praise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a> here. These hymns, the Thirumurai, were stored on palm leaf in the temple and recovered by the Chola king Rajaraja Chola under the guidance of Nambiandarnambi.</p><p>Most inscriptions in the temple date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja Chola I</a> (985 to 1014) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-chola-iii">Rajaraja Chola III</a> (1216 to 1256), with others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>, Pallava and Vijayanagara kings. The Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parantaka-i">Parantaka I</a> (907 to 950) laid the golden roof of the Chitrambalam, and Rajaraja Chola I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulothunga Chola I</a> (1070 to 1120) made significant donations. Court historians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> state that in 1311 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> raided Chidambaram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a> and other temple towns and destroyed temples that held gold and jewels. The temple complex covers about 40 acres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Thillai Nataraja Temple complex holds shrines to Shivakami Amman, Ganesh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> as Govindaraja Perumal, one of the Divya Desams. Its main festivals are the Marghazhi Thiruvaadhirai in December and January and the Aani Thirumanjanam in June and July, when the festival deity is taken out in a procession with a temple car. Natyanjali, a festival of bharatanatyam dancers, takes place each February. Annamalai University, founded in 1929, stands in the town, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 62,153 people in the municipality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chidambaram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society was organised around temples, Brahmin assemblies, agricultural villages and merchant communities, and the kings lavished wealth on the great temples of Madurai, Srirangam and <b>Chidambaram</b>, where Saiva and Vaishnava devotion was central to public life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that Rajaraja I of the Chola dynasty (985–1013 CE), hearing short excerpts at his court, set out to recover them, and that the priest Nambiyandar Nambi found the palm leaves, half eaten by white ants, in a chamber of the <b>Chidambaram</b> Nataraja temple.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tevaram">Tevaram</a></li></ul></section>
